With these objectives, the Master's in Human-Computer Interaction is structured into three strongly interconnected learning areas:
- Socio-cognitive area, with themes of mind-brain interaction and cognitive constraints, enabling and rehabilitative technologies, social interaction, inclusive design;
- Computer science area, with themes of user-centered design, participatory design, human-computer interaction, prototyping of interactive systems, affective computing, e-learning;
- Methodological area, with themes of quantitative and qualitative research methodology, visual design, ethics and epistemology of design. The internship can be carried out in one of the University's laboratories with an academic supervisor, or at companies, with an academic supervisor and a company supervisor. The thesis has a dual purpose: assessment of acquired skills and theoretical-conceptual deepening of topics related to cognitive aspects of interaction design, typically accompanied by an empirical component.

